he's won many races in his career but  this one was special for joe appear  his return to fitness after cancer  he was diagnosed with prostate cancer  last july but told he'd have to wait for  surgery  i mean that was a huge  blow for me and that was because of the  issues with the pandemic and the waiting  list by the time he had the operation in  september the cancer had already  progressed from stage two to stage three  i think that's the one of the most scary  is that you've got something inside you  that's growing that could potentially  could be fatal  so for me i just wanted it out  and it was very scary  and you can see that there's so many  people that have not gone forward  because of the pandemic  and there's so many people even if  they've gone forward they've not been  able to get seen because of the pandemic  as the country went into lockdown  millions of people missed out on vital  cancer screening  covered patient numbers surged and  referrals for suspected cancer fell  dramatically  but over the last year a record-breaking  2.7 million people have been referred  for cancer checks of those 315 000 have  started treatment for cancer but there  are still 30 000 people who haven't yet  started treatment due to the pandemic  the nhs says mobile clinics and  one-stop-shop testing sites are making  it easier to get a diagnosis  but gps say patients are still facing  delays the first step of things like  scans or blood tests or perhaps  biopsies or endoscopies that's the first  step and there is a waiting list there  and then the second step is if cancer is  confirmed then they need to see a  specialist  cancer team an oncology team and there's  also weightiness there as well so the  good news is patients are coming forward  the challenge for the nhs is to make  sure that we manage to meet that demand  a demand that will only be met by  recruiting more staff we really need to  make sure that as well as clearing the  current backlog that we've got the right  numbers of doctors nurses oncologists to  provide that care and support in the  future  joe now urges anyone with concerns to  get checked and feels fortunate his  cancer was treated in time  becky johnson sky news
 

